Description: antinomie: debug engine: follow fork, vfork and exec, non-stop threads, adoptable sessions
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-20

Several sessions can exist at once (a list, not one pointer), each over one thread group, and the engine's bytes live in an address-space object a vfork child shares with its parent until it execs. On top of that:  Fork and vfork (option FORK). A fork of the program stops the parent thread at the syscall return with the child's identity and opens a session for the child, parked before its first user instruction and holding a record of the engine bytes it inherited (taken out when the debugger adopts it). A vfork parent sleeps in the kernel and cannot park: its stop is reported where it is, the thread counts as stopped, and the end of the vfork is a VFORK_DONE event. A clone that shares memory without being a vfork has no owner and is not followed. A child nobody adopts when its parent session ends runs on free.  Exec (option EXEC). The session continues in the new image: breakpoints and debug registers are dropped, the thread that exec'd is the group leader, the event carries the path. One-shot events (fork, exec, vfork done) that arrive while a stop cannot take them are reported when the debugger resumes, before anything runs.  Non-stop (option NONSTOP). A thread with an event parks alone and the event goes to a queue the debugger reads and acknowledges; the others run. Software breakpoint bytes stay in memory, memory and breakpoint requests need no stop, a write over a byte keeps it, and THREAD_CTL stops, continues or steps one thread.  Steps no longer plant a temporary breakpoint. A system call ends its step at its exit (the syscall_exit_work hook ptrace's single step uses, taken before the SIGTRAP report), and a repeated string instruction traps in place until it has finished. That is what lets threads step independently.  Reconnect and restored snapshots. The control fence no longer ends sessions, so a session, its breakpoints and its stopped threads survive; SESSIONS, BP_LIST and ATTACH ADOPT let a host find and take them over.  Phase 2 limits: a thread created while the group runs now gets the program's debug registers from its own context before its first instruction (the clone hook), and STOP_RUN takes the engine's bytes out even when the group could not be stopped.

